Here's the code from shk.c near line 2038:

	/* shopkeeper may notice if the player isn't very knowledgeable -
	   especially when gem prices are concerned */
	if (!obj->dknown || !objects[obj->otyp].oc_name_known) {
		if (obj->oclass =3D=3D GEM_CLASS) {
			/* different shop keepers give different prices */
			if (objects[obj->otyp].oc_material =3D=3D GEMSTONE ||
			    objects[obj->otyp].oc_material =3D=3D GLASS) {
				tmp =3D (obj->otyp % (6 - shkp->m_id % 3));
				tmp =3D (tmp + 3) * obj->quan;
			}

If I read this code correctly, it applies if you haven't identified
that
specific gem (ID scroll on it or ID scroll on a different gem of that
type plus BUC at an altar) and if you have identified the type with
an ID scroll or touchstone but you haven't done a BUC on this
one.  After all, gems are generated uncursed except loadstones
and stuff in bones levels.  The shopkeeper will give a token 3
zorkmids for glass, or a reduced version of its value for a valuable
gem.

I originally thought something like this -

If I use a unicorn to know for certain that a type of gem is glass, I
can still sell it in a shop for 3 zorkmids, but if I ID the type for
real
I can only get 0 zorkmids for it.

If I use a unicorn to know for certain it's a valuable gem (directly
